<section id="what-is-yaml" class="py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-50">
    What Is YAML?
  </h2>
  <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
    <p class="text-lg text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
      YAML stands for <strong class="text-zinc-800 dark:text-zinc-200">YAML Ain't Markup Language</strong>. It is a human-readable data serialization format designed to be easy to write and read. Unlike JSON or XML, YAML uses <strong class="text-zinc-800 dark:text-zinc-200">indentation</strong> (spaces, never tabs) to represent structure, making it the preferred choice for configuration files across the DevOps and cloud-native ecosystem.
    </p>
    <p>
      Created in 2001 by Clark Evans, YAML was designed with one goal: <em>make configuration files readable by humans first, machines second</em>. Every YAML file is a tree of key-value pairs, lists, and nested objects — expressed through whitespace, colons, and dashes rather than brackets and commas.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today, YAML powers the configuration layer of nearly every major DevOps tool: Kubernetes manifests, Docker Compose files, Ansible playbooks, GitHub Actions workflows, CI/CD pipelines, OpenAPI specifications, and Helm charts all speak YAML. If you work with cloud infrastructure or automation, you work with YAML every day.
    </p>
    <p>
      The format's biggest strength — meaningful whitespace — is also its biggest pain point. A single missing space, a tab where spaces are expected, or a misaligned indentation can break an entire deployment pipeline. that's exactly why a reliable YAML formatter is an essential tool. Learn more in our <a href='/blog/yaml-syntax-guide' class='text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 hover:underline'>complete YAML syntax guide</a> in every developer's toolkit.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
